/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186540939 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540992 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "7b2948cb-066b-5028-aa6d-bf771aec3f1b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540994 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186540930 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540994 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540994 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540994 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186540930 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540994 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "7cb8eed7-dd98-5bd2-8807-1eee6e64325d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541010 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186540810 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541010 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541010 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541010 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186540810 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541010 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "9ea07dac-1cdf-58ab-b138-fc3a110b4291" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541016 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186540715 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541016 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541016 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541016 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186540715 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541016 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "b1a61518-8249-5d82-89b0-dd7a0d81fab9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541026 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186540541 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541026 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186540570 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541026 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186540624 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541026 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186540641 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541026 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186540735 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541026 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541026 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541026 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186540641 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541026 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "bfcd94c6-4a82-5d36-a7d1-d10e9ebad35a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541085 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186540946 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541085 #edb/ref 1069 "q-138966" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541085 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541085 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx={e}^{t},\\,y=2{e}^{t/2},\\,t\\in [0,4].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541085 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5b66c4d7-d9b1-5cb6-840d-18a9a3471ea0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541085 #edb/ref 1074 "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=4.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})={e}^{t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{e}^{t/2})={e}^{t/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}2{e}^{t/2}\\sqrt{{({e}^{t})}^{2}+{({e}^{t/2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}{e}^{t/2}\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+{e}^{t}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}{e}^{t/2}\\sqrt{{e}^{t}({e}^{t}+1)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}{e}^{t/2}\\cdot {e}^{t/2}\\cdot \\sqrt{{e}^{t}+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{{e}^{t}+1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541098 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186541016 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541098 #edb/ref 1069 "q-353478" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541098 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541098 #edb/ref 1071 "The number $y(t)$ of people who have adopted electric vehicles in a town follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=4200y-7{y}^{2},\\,y(0)=55,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in years, since the observations began. According to this model, what is the maximum possible number of people who will adopt electric vehicles?\n\nThe maximum possible number of people who will adopt electric vehicles is\n{{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541098 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "70edaacc-f97d-53a0-9bf0-62992d59dc46" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541098 #edb/ref 1074 "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity. \n\nThe carrying capacity $a$ represents the maximum possible number of people who will adopt electric vehicles. A diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](images/d8/d8133f1c4203903bac606bbae61c73717e2ba2dc15a97a77f48eaaeaf4736d06.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =4200y-7{y}^{2} \\\\  & =7y(600-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=600.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $600,$ and we conclude that the maximum possible number of people who will adopt electric vehicles is $600.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541143 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186541026 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541143 #edb/ref 1069 "q-109259" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541143 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541143 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/d9/d9ad43f4bddaeab8327e5405193471c005962013355ec84c4a6f858640216dd7.png)\n\nConsider the arc of the curve $x=-{y}^{2}-5y-4$ that lies in the fourth quadrant between its $y$-intercepts. What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541143 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dd63787d-4bed-5aec-9b0b-525bc4b676f9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541143 #edb/ref 1074 "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we find where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{y}^{2}-5y-4 & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}+5y+4 & =0 \\\\ (y+1)(y+4) & =0 \\\\ y & =-4,-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $y=-4$ to $y=-1.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(-{y}^{2}-5y-4)=-2y-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-4}^{-1}(-{y}^{2}-5y-4)\\sqrt{1+{(-2y-5)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-2\\pi {\\int }_{-4}^{-1}({y}^{2}+5y+4)\\sqrt{26+20y+4{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-2\\pi \\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{-4}^{-1}({y}^{2}+5y+4)\\sqrt{2{y}^{2}+10y+13}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541150 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186540957 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541150 #edb/ref 1069 "q-344647" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541150 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541150 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the average value ${g}_{\\text{avg}}$ of the function $g(x)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ on the interval $[-\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4}].$ Do not round the answer.\n\n${g}_{\\text{avg}}=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541150 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f3134f95-ccde-508a-8ade-bbcbdfc93226" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541150 #edb/ref 1074 "Applying the average value formula, we find that the average value of the function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{\\text{avg}} & =\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\pi /4-(-\\pi /4))}{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{2}{\\pi }(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)|}_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }[(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}))-(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{3\\pi }{4})-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}))] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }[(2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-4\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})-(2\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})-4\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}))] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }[(-\\sqrt{2})-(\\sqrt{2})] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\cdot (-2\\sqrt{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{-4\\sqrt{2}}{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541156 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186541189 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541156 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "06b90397-7e54-5498-8b04-22ca8c09726a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541156 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186360827 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541168 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186541156 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541168 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2